winewayland: verify sibling validity for OWNED windows in the z-order mirror

Premiere died with NO Wine backtrace -- the log simply stops mid-frame -- whenever the user
clicked away from the app with NEUTRON_OWNED_ZORDER=1. The only evidence is the compositor's:

    wl_display#1: error 1: invalid arguments for xdg_popup#222.reposition
    kwin_wayland: invalid object (84), type (wl_surface), message reposition(3ou)
    kwin_wayland: error in client communication (pid 2445092)

⭐ A FATAL WAYLAND PROTOCOL ERROR PRODUCES NO WINE-LEVEL CRASH EVIDENCE. Nothing in the wine log
names it; `journalctl --user` and the `wl_display#1: error` line are all there is. The reported
message is also NOT necessarily the culprit -- a malformed request corrupts the stream and the
compositor reports the mis-parse of a LATER, well-formed one. Two hours went into the popup
reposition path, which turned out to be entirely correct.

CAUSE. neutron_restack_apply() sources owned windows from wayland_owned_subsurfaces_of(), which
matches on wayland_surface->toplevel_hwnd -- an HWND. An HWND stays equal across a
destroy/recreate of the toplevel's wl_surface (defocus, docking, any role change), so the
subsurface remained a child of the OLD wl_surface while `prev` in the placement loop was a sibling
of the NEW one. wl_subsurface.place_above against a non-sibling is fatal.

The client-surface loop twenty lines above already guards exactly this:

    if (cs->toplevel_wl_surface != tsurf->wl_surface) continue; /* not our sibling */

The owned loop had no equivalent -- it ASSERTED the relationship instead of checking it:

    owned_slots[n].toplevel_wl_surface = tsurf->wl_surface;

FIX: record subsurface_parent (the actual parent wl_surface) in wayland_surface_make_subsurface(),
clear it in wayland_surface_clear_role() with the rest of the role, and compare it in the owned
loop. An HWND cannot answer a question about wl_surface identity; only the pointer can.

MEASURED: crash 2/2 in the configuration (owned-subsurface-captioned +
NEUTRON_SYSCOMMAND_DECLINE=1 + owned-zorder), 0/2 after, same gestures. With the mirror OFF the
same session never crashed, which is what isolated it.

⚠️ SEPARATE, STILL OPEN: this does not fix owned windows sinking behind the Program Monitor. The
mirror computes orders containing only ONE OR TWO surfaces (9 restack applications against 1369
owned promotions in one session) and the Program Monitor is absent from them, so the plugin is
correctly placed on top of a stack that does not contain the surface it needs to be above. The
client-surface loop's own staleness guard silently DROPS such surfaces instead of crashing --
same root class, opposite symptom. Next step is to log the skip reason in that loop.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
@@ -402,6 +402,11 @@
         {
             struct wl_subsurface *wl_subsurface;
             HWND toplevel_hwnd;
+            /* NEUTRON: the wl_surface this subsurface is ACTUALLY a child of. toplevel_hwnd is an
+             * HWND and survives the parent's wl_surface being destroyed and recreated, so it
+             * cannot answer "is this still a sibling of that surface?" -- and place_above against
+             * a non-sibling is a FATAL protocol error that kills the connection. */
+            struct wl_surface *subsurface_parent;
         };
     };
 
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
@@ -615,6 +615,7 @@
 
     surface->role = WAYLAND_SURFACE_ROLE_SUBSURFACE;
     surface->toplevel_hwnd = parent->hwnd;
+    surface->subsurface_parent = parent->wl_surface;
 
     /* Present contents independently of the parent surface. */
     wl_subsurface_set_desync(surface->wl_subsurface);
@@ -840,6 +841,7 @@
         }
 
         surface->toplevel_hwnd = 0;
+        surface->subsurface_parent = NULL;
         break;
     }
 
@@ -1890,6 +1892,7 @@
     UINT count;
 };
 
+
 /* ---- NEUTRON round-36 TEMPORAL SUPPRESSION (issues: CEF/CEP blank + invisible-
  * until-hover). Windows composes bitblt presents and GDI paints TEMPORALLY into
  * one surface: the latest painter wins per pixel. Native subsurfaces break that:
@@ -2439,6 +2442,15 @@
         {
             struct wayland_win_data *od = wayland_win_data_get_nolock(owned[k]);
             if (!od || !od->wayland_surface || !od->wayland_surface->wl_subsurface) continue;
+            /* NEUTRON sibling-validity, matching the client-surface loop above. Selection came
+             * from toplevel_hwnd, an HWND -- which stays equal across a destroy/recreate of the
+             * toplevel's wl_surface (defocus, docking, any role change). The subsurface is then
+             * still a child of the OLD surface while `prev` below is a sibling of the NEW one,
+             * and wl_subsurface.place_above against a non-sibling is FATAL: kwin answers
+             * "error in client communication" and Premiere dies with no Wine backtrace at all.
+             * MEASURED 2026-08-27: crashes on clicking off the app, only with NEUTRON_OWNED_ZORDER
+             * on; identical gate set minus the mirror survives the same gesture. */
+            if (od->wayland_surface->subsurface_parent != tsurf->wl_surface) continue;
             memset(&owned_slots[n], 0, sizeof(owned_slots[n]));
             owned_slots[n].wl_subsurface = od->wayland_surface->wl_subsurface;
             owned_slots[n].wl_surface = od->wayland_surface->wl_surface;
